\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 3. The Pathological Nature of the Postulate 
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $H'$\uc0\u8236 \
\pard\tx680\li680\fi-480\slleading120\sa174\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs40 \cf2 \'95	
\f0\b\fs32 The Claim:
\f1\b0  The candidate prediction of a linewidth-dependent gravitational Bell degradation (\'a76) requires the addition of a non-standard coupling Hamiltonian: 
\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $H' \\sim g\\,(\\mathbf\{d\}\\cdot\\hat\{\\mathbf\{H\}\})\\,\\phi_\{\\text\{bulk\}\}$
\f3\fs32 \cf2 \uc0\u8236  
\f1 .
\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx680\li680\fi-480\slleading120\sa164\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs40 \cf2 \'95	
\f0\b\fs32 The Failure:
\f1\b0  This postulate is not merely "non-covariant"; it is structurally pathological within gauge theories. The absolute bulk phase 
\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $\\phi_\{\\text\{bulk\}\}$
\fs32 \cf2 \uc0\u8236  is a local 
\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $U(1)$
\fs32 \cf2 \uc0\u8236  gauge degree of freedom. Coupling an explicit observable (the polarization projection) directly to an absolute gauge phase violates global charge conservation and gauge invariance. Furthermore, because 
\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $\\phi_\{\\text\{bulk\}\}$
\fs32 \cf2 \uc0\u8236  is identified as an electromagnetically maintained reference, forcing it to couple directly to a polarization vector breaks the rotational symmetry of the electromagnetic dipole interaction without a spontaneous symmetry-breaking mechanism.
\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx680\li680\fi-480\slleading120\sa174\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs40 \cf2 \'95	
\f0\b\fs32 Fix:
\f1\b0  The text must explicitly state that 
\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $H'$
\fs32 \cf2 \uc0\u8236  violates standard gauge principles. The author should frame the proposed experiment not as a test of an alternative theory, but as a rigid boundary setting the extreme limits where MCI's core assumption (that absolute phase differences of the bulk are physically accessible) breaks down.
\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\slleading120\sb340\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 4. Spontaneous Breaking of Lorentz Invariance via the Cut
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx680\li680\fi-480\slleading120\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs40 \cf2 \'95	
\f0\b\fs32 The Claim:
\f1\b0  Lorentz invariance is not broken fundamentally, but "spontaneously, by the measurement event, which selects the bulk frame" (\'a78).
\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx680\li680\fi-480\slleading120\sa174\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs40 \cf2 \'95	
\f0\b\fs32 The Failure:
\f1\b0  In standard quantum field theory, spontaneous symmetry breaking (SSB) implies that the underlying Lagrangian is invariant under a symmetry group, but the vacuum state is not. Here, the assertion is fundamentally different: individual local measurement interactions are said to couple to a physical rest frame (the bulk/vacuum rest frame), while the in-flight propagation remains covariant. If a localized boundary interaction responds to an absolute ambient velocity vector, the underlying local interaction Hamiltonian 
\f2\i itself
\f1\i0  must contain explicit background-frame vectors (e.g., a preferred four-velocity 
\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8237 $u^\\mu$
\fs32 \cf2 \uc0\u8236 ). This is explicit symmetry breaking, not spontaneous breaking.
\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx680\li680\fi-480\slleading120\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs40 \cf2 \'95	
\f0\b\fs32 Fix:
\f1\b0  Correct the terminology. Admit that the Vacuum Preferred-Frame Hypothesis (VPFH) introduces an explicit, low-energy Lorentz-violating background tensor into the boundary-interaction sector, and that the framework relies on gravitational or cosmological suppression factors to evade precision tests.
\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\slleading120\sb340\sa120\partightenfactor0

